  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "**This is the easy version of the problem. The only difference is that in the easy version, NNN is guaranteed to be equal to MMM.**\n\nYou are given two arrays AAA and BBB, of lengths NNN and MMM respectively, each consisting of positive integers.\n\n**It is guaranteed that N=MN = MN=M**.\n\nYour score is initially 000.\n\nYou can perform the following operation any number of times, as long as both arrays AAA and BBB are non-empty:\n\n\nSelect an integer XXX from AAA, and an integer YYY from BBB, such that X≠YX \\neq YX=Y.\nRemove one occurrence of XXX from AAA, and one occurrence of YYY from BBB.\nAdd (X+Y)(X + Y)(X+Y) to your score.\nYour task is to find the maximum possible score that you can obtain.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT — the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of three lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two integers NNN and MMM — the sizes of arrays AAA and BBB, respectively.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nThe third line contains MMM space-separated integers B1,B2,…,BMB_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_MB1​,B2​,…,BM​.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the maximum score you can get.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤3⋅1041 \\le T \\le 3 \\cdot 10^41≤T≤3⋅104",
      "1≤N,M≤2⋅1051 \\le N, M \\le 2 \\cdot 10^51≤N,M≤2⋅105",
      "N=MN = MN=M",
      "1≤Ai,Bi≤1091 \\le A_i, B_i \\le 10^91≤Ai​,Bi​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105.",
      "The sum of MMM over all test cases does not exceed 2⋅1052 \\cdot 10^52⋅105."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3 3\n7 7 7\n7 7 7\n3 3\n7 7 9\n9 7 9\n2 2\n7 7\n7 9",
        "output": "0\n48\n16",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: A=[7,7,7]A = [7, 7, 7]A=[7,7,7] and B=[7,7,7]B = [7, 7, 7]B=[7,7,7].\nAll the elements of AAA and BBB are equal, so we cannot choose two different elements at all. The score is 000.\nTest case 222: A=[7,7,9]A = [7, 7, 9]A=[7,7,9] and B=[9,7,9]B = [9, 7, 9]B=[9,7,9]. One optimal sequence of operations is as follows:\n\nChoose 999 from AAA and 777 from BBB.\nThe score increases by 9+7=169+7 = 169+7=16. The arrays become A=[7,7]A = [7, 7]A=[7,7] and B=[9,9]B = [9, 9]B=[9,9].\nNext, choose 777 from AAA and 999 from BBB.\nThe score increases by 9+7=169+7 = 169+7=16. The arrays become A=[7]A = [7]A=[7] and B=[9]B = [9]B=[9].\nAgain, choose 777 from AAA and 999 from BBB.\nThe score increases by 9+7=169+7 = 169+7=16. The arrays both become empty.\n\nThe total score is 16+16+16=4816+16+16=4816+16+16=48, and this is optimal.\nTest case 333: A=[7,7]A = [7, 7]A=[7,7] and B=[7,9]B = [7, 9]B=[7,9]. On the first move, choose a 777 from AAA and a 999 from BBB, to increase the score by 7+9=167+9 = 167+9=16.\nThe arrays then become A=[7]A = [7]A=[7] and B=[7]B = [7]B=[7]. It's no longer possible to choose two different elements, so we stop here.\nThe score is 161616, and this is optimal."
      }
    ]
